---
import fs from "node:fs";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { atlas as idx } from "../lib/atlas.js";
import full from "../data/places-full.json";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
/* EVERY NUMBER ON THIS PAGE IS COUNTED, not typed. The page says what the map
   does not know, and a hand-written figure on it goes stale the first time a
   harvest runs — which is exactly the failure this page exists to catch. */
const dots = idx.places.length;
const hr = full.places.filter((p) => p.country === "HR");
const places = hr.length;
const volumes = hr.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.collections || []).length, 0);
const walked = full.places.filter((p) => p.via !== "geonames-admin"
                                      && (p.collections || []).length).length;
const countries = new Set(full.places.map((p) => p.country).filter(Boolean)).size;
const walkedCC = new Set(full.places.filter((p) => p.via !== "geonames-admin"
                                      && (p.collections || []).length)
                                    .map((p) => p.country)).size;
const regionList = regions.regions.map((r) => r.label).join(", ");

/* The chapters this page had not caught up with, all counted the same way.
   A story about an atlas is a claim about its contents, so the claims are
   read out of the contents. */
const eras = regions.regions.reduce((a, r) => a + r.eras.length, 0);

/* THE DENOMINATOR, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE «what it does not know»
   SECTION. `a` is an index into idx.rank; anything that is not one of the
   seven surveyed classes is ground nobody has walked. */
const RANK = idx.rank || [];
const surveyedClasses = new Set(["free", "account", "index", "mixed", "paid",
                                 "catalogue", "onsite"]);
const accessOf = (p) => (typeof p.a === "number" && RANK[p.a]) || "unsurveyed";
const surveyed = idx.places.filter((p) => surveyedClasses.has(accessOf(p))).length;
const unsurveyedPct = Math.round((1 - surveyed / dots) * 1000) / 10;

let trove = { counts: { titles: 0 } };
try { trove = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("../data/trove-newspapers.json", "utf8")); }
catch { /* the newspapers are optional; the sentence about them is not printed
           when they are absent rather than printed as a zero. */ }
const troveTitles = trove.counts?.titles || 0;

let flagPages = 0;
try { flagPages = fs.readdirSync("src/flags-pages").filter((f) => f.endsWith(".html") && f !== "index.html").length; }
catch { flagPages = 0; }
---
<Base
  title="About Record Atlas"
  eyebrow="What this is, where it came from, and what it does not know"
  dek="Where genealogical records are, who holds them and what it costs to look. Search by any name a place has ever had.">

  <div class="k-hi">
    <p>My great-grandfather was born in Gallignana in 1863: where is that record, and can I see it tonight?</p>
    <p>That one question is what this atlas answers. It began as one family's archive and was opened to the world. It is early, and it says so.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="k-stats" aria-label="The atlas in numbers">
    <span><b>{dots.toLocaleString()}</b> places</span>
    <span><b>{countries}</b> countries</span>
    <span><b>{regions.regions.length}</b> record regions</span>
    <span><b>{eras.toLocaleString()}</b> eras</span>
    <span class="k-tag is-new">{unsurveyedPct}% not yet surveyed</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h">Four things stand between you and the record</h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles ab-tiles">
    <li><b>The name changed</b><span>Gallignana is Gračišće. Fiume is Rijeka. The find box matches every name a place has had, for the whole world: 45,985 places and 115,922 other names.</span></li>
    <li class="is-link"><b>The jurisdiction changed</b><span>A 1930 record from the same village may be filed under <em>Italy</em>. It is the commonest reason a record cannot be found.
      <a href={u("/jurisdictions/")}>See the table →</a></span></li>
    <li><b>“Online” is eight different things</b><span>Free images, a login, an index only, part paid, paywall, a catalogue card, in the building, and for nine places in ten, nobody has looked. The colour of the dot says which.</span></li>
    <li><b>The record is not always a register</b><span>Australia has no parish registers. {troveTitles.toLocaleString()} digitised newspapers are the record for most ancestors before 1950.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h">Whose flag flew over it</h2>
  <div class="k-hi is-plain">
    <p>{flagPages} pages of flag history say who governed which ground, and when. This atlas knows which archive each regime’s paper went to, for {eras.toLocaleString()} eras in {regions.regions.length} regions.</p>
    <p>The two sit side by side and are not yet one search. <a href={u("/flags/")}>Open the flags →</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h">Where it came from</h2>
  <div class="k-hi is-plain">
    <p>Out of <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheDefranceski/map/">one family’s archive</a>. Its Croatian depth ({places} places, {volumes.toLocaleString()} volumes) is that archive’s work, imported once. What was family-specific was dropped, including its colouring by how far one researcher had got.</p>
    <p>There are <a href={u("/my-work/")}>nine such archives</a>, and what they share is why this atlas exists.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h">What it does not know</h2>
  <div class="k-hi">
    <p><b>{unsurveyedPct}% of the ground on this map has never been surveyed.</b></p>
    <p>Of {dots.toLocaleString()} dots in {countries} countries, only {walked.toLocaleString()} (in {walkedCC} countries) have been walked volume by volume. A grey dot means nobody has looked, never that nothing is there.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="k-tiles ab-tiles">
    <li><b>Regions follow the research</b><span>Each was written because someone was stuck on that ground and needed to know whose archive to write to.</span></li>
    <li><b>Unopened sources are listed as such</b><span>Over three hundred sources are listed because a register or open dataset named them. Two hundred have had their door tried; none is called surveyed until someone has read it.</span></li>
    <li><b>Declined after looking is a result</b><span>Fifteen sources were assessed and turned down with the reason written out. A bot gate is never worked around.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <details class="k-more ab-more">
    <summary><span class="k-mt">The longer account</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The regions run {regionList} rather than the tidy sweep of Europe you might expect, because they were chosen by where research is being done, not by what would look impressive on a map. A place outside a mapped region says so in its own panel rather than guessing.</p>
      <p>Keeping “nobody has surveyed it” apart from “nothing is there” is worth more than coverage: a map that quietly implies completeness sends people home when they should keep looking.</p>
      <p>An archive that refuses a robot is stating terms, and an atlas that asks archives to trust it cannot be the thing that ignores them.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 class="k-h">How it is built</h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles ab-tiles">
    <li><b>A static site</b><span>No server, no database, no account. The data is plain JSON <a href={idx.repo || "https://github.com/daviddef/TheMap"}>in a public repository</a>.</span></li>
    <li><b>Gated before it ships</b><span>The build refuses a source with no date, a provider that does not resolve, or coordinates in the wrong country. That last gate caught the Carnia comuni filed as Croatian.</span></li>
    <li><b>Downloaded, not scraped</b><span>Country outlines are Natural Earth, the gazetteer is GeoNames (CC BY 4.0), maps are OpenStreetMap. Historical regions are drawn by hand, roughly, to say which filing system applied, not to settle a border.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="k-foot">
    <p>Behind the atlas</p>
    <p><a href={u("/worklist/")}>What is being done</a> · <a href={u("/errands/")}>Letters to send</a> · <a href={u("/my-work/")}>My work</a> · <a href={u("/changelog/")}>What changed</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .ab-tiles { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); max-width: 980px; }
  .ab-tiles li > span { display: block; }
  .ab-tiles li b { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17px; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
  .ab-tiles li a { font-size: 13px; color: var(--accent); }
  .ab-more { margin: -8px 0 26px; max-width: 78ch; }
  .ab-more .prose p { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .k-stats > span { white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
